{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Ten unchecked steps at 85% each leaves only about 20% correct, and the AI can't check itself, so you need a separate check that you pick by scoring candidates on known answers, not by opinion.","claims":["When a task has many steps in a row, small error rates multiply into a mostly wrong result, and the AI can't reliably grade its own work. Put a separate check after the steps, and choose that check by trying candidates on examples where you already know the right answers, then keep the one that scores best rather than the one that seems best to you.","When a task has many steps in a row, small error rates multiply into a mostly wrong result. The AI can't reliably catch its own mistakes, so add a separate check after the steps. Choose that check by running candidate checks on examples where you already know the right answer and keeping the one that scores best, not the one that seems best to you.","When an AI does a long chain of tasks, small error rates in each step multiply into a mostly wrong result, and the AI can't reliably judge its own work. Add a separate checking step, and choose which checker to use by running the candidates on examples where you already know the right answers and keeping the one that scores best, not the one that seems best to you.","When a task has many steps in a row, small error rates at each step multiply into a mostly-wrong result, so don't trust the AI's own review of its work. Put a separate checker after the steps, and choose which checker to use by running the candidates on examples where you already know the right answer and keeping the one that scores best, not the one that seems best to you."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The claim's action is to build a separate, repeatable check and choose it by scoring candidates against known answers instead of by feel. That is the eval idea. H is about safety checks, and the claim isn't about safety.","The claim is about using a separate judge that is checked against known-answer examples, not the agent's self-grade, so you can measure what works. That is the eval idea. H is about safety, which the claim doesn't mention.","The claim's core is that the agent can't reliably judge its own output, so you add a separate check and run it. It doesn't say to compare changes over time with a fixed judge, which is what C is about.","The claim's core action is picking a checker by scoring candidates against known-right answers, which is measuring output against defined criteria with a judge you've validated. It isn't about safety (E) or grounding (A).","The actionable core is picking the checker by scoring candidates against known right answers, which is a repeatable eval with a fixed yardstick. It isn't grounding in truth (A), and it isn't mainly 'run a check because output can't show safety' (C).","The actionable core is picking the checker by measuring candidates against known-answer examples instead of by judgment, which is the eval idea. It is not grounding, because there is no stay-connected-to-truth or gap-filling theme. It is not safety, because the topic is correctness.","The actionable core is choosing the checker by scoring candidates on known-answer examples instead of by gut feel, which is an eval with a fixed judge.","Choosing the checker by scoring candidates against known-right examples is the eval idea: a fixed judge measured against criteria, repeatably, to tell what improves the work."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't let the AI vouch for itself. Test the checks on your own material, score them, and open the file instead of trusting its summary, because ten steps at 85% each leaves you at about 20%. I never saw a cost figure, though.","claims":["When an AI agent reports that its work is done or correct, don't take its word. Open the actual output files yourself, and build and run your own tests of the checks on material you know, scoring how often they catch real problems. Small error rates at each step multiply across a long chain of steps, so a chain that looks fine step by step can fail most of the time overall.","When an AI reports it finished a task or passed a check, don't take its word. Try the checks yourself on your own data, put a number on how well they work, and read the actual output files, because small error rates at each step multiply across a long chain and leave the final result mostly unreliable.","When an AI reports that its work is done or correct, don't take its word for it. Build your own tests for its checks using your own data, give them scores, and open the actual output yourself, because small error rates compound across many steps (ten steps at 85% each leaves about a 20% chance the whole chain is right).","When an AI agent reports that its work is done or correct, don't take its word. Build your own checks, try them on material you know, give them a score, and look inside the actual output files. Small error rates at each step multiply across a long chain of steps, so a process that looks reliable at each step can fail most of the time overall."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Don't trust the agent's self-report; run your own checks on known material and measure how often they catch problems (compounding error is the supporting reason), matching 'run the check, not wait for certainty'.","Don't trust the agent's own 'done' report; run your own checks and measure how well they catch problems, since output alone can't show safety or correctness.","Claim says don't trust the AI's own report of correctness; build your own checks, score them, open the output. That matches 'can't tell safety from output, run the check', not eval-judge-fixed (G) or grounding (D).","The claim is about not trusting the agent's self-report and running your own checks, tests and inspecting the output, which is the 'run the check' idea (A) rather than a fixed-judge eval (F).","Don't trust the AI's self-reported pass; run the checks yourself and verify the actual outputs, which is 'you can't tell it's safe from the output, run the check'.","The colleague would verify the agent's claims by running the check and reading the output files, not trusting its report, which is E's idea that you can't judge from the output and have to run the check.","Don't trust the agent's own 'done' report; build and run your own checks and inspect the real output, because you can't judge safety or correctness from the output alone.","Not trusting the agent's own 'done' report and running your own checks on known material to verify output matches A's 'you can't tell from output, run the check'; the compounding-error point is supporting detail, not a different idea."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust a checking method on faith: test the candidates on your own material, read the scoreboard, and keep the one that wins and writes down its limit. I'd still want to know what it costs to run before taking it to the board.","claims":["Before adopting any method for checking your work, run each candidate on your own real examples, compare their results side by side, pick the one that scores best, and record where it breaks down. Also find out what it costs to run before proposing it to leadership.","When choosing between ways to check an AI's work, don't just pick one that sounds good: run each candidate on our own real examples, compare their results side by side, and adopt the best one, noting where it doesn't work well. Also find out how much it costs to run before proposing it to leadership.","Before adopting any way of checking your agent's work, run each candidate on your own real examples, compare their results side by side, and pick the best performer. Record where that method stops working, and find out what it costs to run before you propose it to leadership.","Before you adopt any way of checking AI output, run each candidate on your own real examples, look at how each one scores, pick the best performer, and record where it stops being reliable. Also find out how much each check costs to run before you propose it to leadership."],"picks":["evaluations","none","none","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["Running candidates on your own real examples, comparing scores side by side and recording failures is measuring output against defined criteria repeatably, which is the eval idea; the cost note is incidental.","The claim is about choosing among candidate checking methods by trying each on real examples, then recording failures and costing the winner. It says nothing about running a check because output can't show safety (C). It also doesn't describe an eval with a fixed judge that measures whether a change improved the work (E).","The claim is about choosing a checking method by running candidates on real examples and picking the winner. That isn't grounding (A), running a check for safety (B), or an eval with a fixed judge measuring change (G).","Running candidate checks on real examples, comparing them side by side and picking the best is the eval idea: measure output against defined criteria repeatably to compare changes.","Running candidate checks on real examples and comparing them side by side to adopt the best is eval-building: measuring output against defined criteria repeatably with a fixed judge.","Running candidate checks on real examples, comparing side by side, and adopting the best one is the eval idea (fixed judge, repeatable measurement of whether work got better); cost and caveats are secondary details.","Testing candidate checks on real examples, scoring them, and noting where they fail is about measuring and validating the measure (eval practice); G's core is repeatable measurement with a fixed judge, though the claim never mentions fixing the judge or measuring changes.","Running candidate checks on real examples, scoring them, picking the best, and noting reliability limits is the eval idea: measuring output against defined criteria repeatably with a fixed judge; cost is an add-on."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Unchecked AI errors compound (85% per step is about 20% over ten), and asking the AI if it's sure is no control. You need an independent check, tested on your own data, with its precision and recall measured. Propose, check, apply is the control I could defend to the board.","claims":["When you let an AI do several steps in a row, assume mistakes pile up: even if each step is right 85% of the time, the whole chain is right only about one time in five. Don't rely on asking the AI whether it is sure. Set up a separate checker, try it on your own real data, and measure how many bad outputs it catches and how often it wrongly flags good ones. Have the AI draft, run the checker, and only then apply the result.","When an AI does a multi-step job, small error rates multiply: at 85% right per step, only about one in five ten-step runs is fully right. Don't trust the AI's own 'yes, I'm sure'. Build a separate checker that has its own view of the work, test it on our real data, and measure how many bad outputs it catches and how many good ones it wrongly flags. Have the AI propose, run the checker, and only then apply, so I can show leadership a control that works.","Before trusting any AI-run multi-step process, build a separate check that doesn't rely on the AI vouching for itself. Test it on your own real examples, count both what it misses and what it wrongly flags, and have it review each step's proposed output before it is applied. Small per-step error rates multiply across steps, so a process that is mostly right at each step is often wrong overall.","Before trusting an AI workflow with many steps, put a separate checker in front of each step's output rather than asking the AI to confirm itself, test that checker on our own real examples, and record how often it catches real mistakes and how often it raises false alarms. Run the workflow as: AI proposes, checker reviews, and only then the result is applied."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Claim says you can't trust the AI's self-assessment; run a separate checker and measure its catch and false-flag rates — that is 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check.'","The claim says you can't trust the agent's self-assessment of correctness, so you build a separate checker, test it on real data, and run it before applying — idea A (you need to run the check, not rely on certainty).","The claim is about running a separate check on each step's output before applying it, instead of trusting the AI's self-confirmation. It is not about measuring whether a change improved the work (H).","A separate checker tested on real examples with measured catch and false-alarm rates, because you can't judge safety from output alone and the practice is running the check.","Building a separate check that doesn't rely on the AI vouching for itself, tested on real examples and run on each step's output, is B's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'; A is about measuring quality changes with a fixed judge, not safety.","The claim says to build an independent check, because the AI vouching for itself can't be trusted, and to run it on every step. That is H's idea of running the check instead of judging from the output.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own 'I'm sure' and to run an independent checker (propose, check, then apply) as a control. That is the 'run the check' idea in H, not eval-based comparison of changes (D).","Core is a separate checker run on real data (catch/false-flag rates) rather than trusting the AI's self-assessment; this is 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check', not an eval for improvement (C) or grounding (B)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"you can't tell if agent output is right just by reading it, so you build a checker and test it. run a few detectors on your own output, score them on precision and recall, keep the winner as a judge file with its blind spots written down. errors compound across steps, so a long pipeline with no checks is mostly wrong.","claims":["When an AI agent hands you work, don't trust it because it reads well. Build a small automated test for it, and try several candidate tests on your own past outputs. Keep the one that catches the most real mistakes without raising false alarms, and write down the kinds of mistakes it still misses. Add checks between the steps of a long chain, because small errors pile up and an unchecked chain ends up mostly wrong.","Don't trust agent output because it reads well. Build several candidate checks, run them against your own real outputs, and measure how often each catches bad results and how often it wrongly flags good ones. Keep the best one as a fixed reviewer, with a written note of what it misses. Put such checks between steps of any multi-step chain, since small errors pile up and an unchecked long chain will mostly end up wrong.","Next week, don't trust your own read of what an AI agent produced. Build a few candidate checkers, run them on output you already know the answer to, and measure how often each misses real errors and how often it raises false alarms. Keep the best one as a standing reviewer, with its known weak spots written down next to it. Put such checks between the steps of a long chain of agent work, because small errors pile up from step to step and an unchecked long chain is mostly wrong by the end.","Don't trust your own read of what an agent produced. Before relying on it, build several candidate checkers, run them on your own outputs where you know the right answer, and measure how often each catches real mistakes and how often it raises false alarms. Keep the best one as a standing reviewer, with a written list of the kinds of mistakes it misses. Put such checks between the steps of any long chain of agent work, because small errors pile up and an unchecked long chain will mostly be wrong."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The claim says not to trust your own read of the output and to run measured checks between chain steps, which is idea E. It doesn't say to compare versions with a fixed judge, which would be G.","The claim says not to trust your own read of agent output, to run measured checks on it, and to place those checks between chain steps. That is idea C. It never says to compare versions of the agent or to hold a judge fixed to see whether a change improved the work, which is what A is about.","The claim says you can't judge agent output by reading it, so you build checkers and run them, which is idea A. It never uses the eval-for-measuring-change idea in D.","The core is that you can't judge an agent's output by looking at it, so you build and run checks (validated on known cases) instead of waiting for certainty. The claim isn't about measuring whether a change improved the work (E), and it isn't about staying connected to ground truth (F).","Building a repeatable test, comparing candidate tests on past outputs, keeping the best one, and noting its misses is the eval idea (fixed criteria and judge, repeatable measure); chain checks are an add-on, not the core.","Building a repeatable test, comparing candidate checks against past outputs, keeping the best, and documenting its blind spots is the eval idea: measure output against fixed criteria so you can judge changes.","Candidate checks measured against real outputs, then a fixed reviewer with known misses, is the eval idea: criteria defined, repeatable, judge fixed. The chain point is an application, not the core.","Building candidate checks, measuring catch and false-flag rates on real outputs, and keeping the best as a fixed reviewer is the eval idea (repeatable criteria, fixed judge); chain checks are an application."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"dont trust one checking method, race a few against each other on your own output, because no single one catches everything and the one i'd have picked first missed stuff. also long unchecked chains rot fast (10 steps at 85% is ~20%), so save the winning check as a judge file with its blind spots written in.","claims":["When you need to verify AI-produced work, don't rely on a single checking approach: run several different checks on the same output and compare which flaws each one finds, because each misses things and your instinct-first pick may be the weakest. Keep the best-performing check as a saved reviewer instruction that also lists what it fails to catch. Don't chain many unreviewed steps together, since small per-step error rates multiply into mostly-wrong results (ten steps at 85% each gives about 20% overall).","When you need to verify your agent's output, build two or three different ways of checking it and run them all on the same real output to see which catches the most problems, rather than going with the one you'd instinctively choose. Then keep the best one as a reusable file for the checker, and list in it what that check tends to miss. Also keep chains of unchecked steps short, since small per-step error rates compound quickly (ten steps at 85% each leaves roughly a 20% chance of a correct result).","When you need to verify your agent's output, don't rely on a single way of checking it. Run several different checks against the same output, compare which ones catch problems, and expect your favorite to miss some. Keep the best check as a saved file that a separate reviewer reads, and write into it the kinds of errors it is known to miss. Also keep chains of unchecked steps short, since small per-step error rates multiply into mostly-wrong results over many steps.","Before trusting any way of checking your agent's work, run several different checks on the same output and compare which problems each one finds, because the check you'd naturally pick first will miss things the others catch. Keep the winning check in a file for reuse, with its known weak spots written down. Also keep unchecked multi-step agent runs short, since small per-step error rates compound badly (ten steps at 85% each leaves only about a 20% chance the whole run is right)."],"picks":["none","none","none","evaluations","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is about running several verification checks and keeping the best one, plus the compounding error of chained steps. That isn't E's grounding idea, B's 'run the check', or H's fixed-judge measurement.","The claim is about comparing several checks and keeping the best one, plus not chaining unreviewed steps. It doesn't carry A's 'run the check, not output-inspection', F's 'fixed judge to measure change', or G's grounding in truth.","The claim is about comparing several checks and keeping the best one, plus keeping unchecked runs short. That is neither grounding (D), nor 'run the check' (C), nor a fixed judge for measuring change (A).","Comparing several checks, keeping the winner in a file with known weak spots is about building a fixed, repeatable eval/judge; the compounding-error point is a side note.","The claim is about running several checks on agent output, knowing each one misses things, and not leaving long chains unchecked. That matches 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It says nothing about tracking whether a change improved the work (H) or staying connected to truth (C).","The claim is about verifying agent output by running checks (several, compared, with a saved reviewer file and a short chain of steps). It says nothing about measuring whether a change improved the work, which is what E is about.","The claim is to verify agent output by building and running checks (several, keep the best, note its blind spots, keep unchecked chains short). It does not say to measure whether a change improved the work (B) or to stay connected to ground truth (E).","The claim is about building and running checks on real output instead of trusting it, and keeping short unchecked chains. It never mentions grounding in outside truth or measuring changes with a fixed judge."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust one check: race several against each other on planted errors and score them on precision, recall and coverage, because none catches everything. And write down what your judge misses.","claims":["When you rely on a checker to catch mistakes, don't pick one and trust it. Plant known mistakes in sample work, run several checkers on it, and compare how many real mistakes each flags, how many of its flags are right, and how much of the ground each covers. Keep a written list of the kinds of mistakes your chosen checker misses, because no single one catches everything.","When you need to catch mistakes automatically, don't rely on a single checker. Deliberately plant known mistakes in sample material, run several different checkers on it, and compare how many real mistakes each one finds, how many false alarms it raises, and how much of the material it covers. Expect every checker to have blind spots, and keep a written list of what yours misses.","When you need to catch mistakes automatically, don't rely on a single checker. Plant known mistakes in sample material, run several different checkers on it side by side, and compare how many real mistakes each flags, how many of its flags are wrong, and how much of the material it covers, since none finds everything. Keep a written list of the kinds of mistakes your chosen checker misses.","When I need to catch mistakes in something, I shouldn't rely on a single reviewer. I'll take several different reviewers, deliberately insert known mistakes into sample material, and run all of them on it. Then I'll measure how many of their flags were real, how many of the planted mistakes each one found, and which kinds of mistakes none of them noticed. I'll also keep a written list of the mistakes my chosen reviewer still misses, so I know where to check by hand."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","security","security","evaluations","none"],"why":["Planting known mistakes and comparing checkers on recall, precision and coverage is measuring and validating the judge/eval itself, which is E's repeatable measurement with a trusted judge.","Planting known mistakes and comparing checkers on precision/recall/coverage is building a fixed, repeatable measurement of the judge itself, which is the eval idea.","Building a test set with planted mistakes and measuring several judges' precision, recall and blind spots is eval practice: criteria you define, repeatable, and a fixed judge so you can tell what works.","Planting known mistakes and measuring flag precision, recall and blind spots across reviewers is building a repeatable, fixed-criteria eval of the judge, which is the eval idea; D is about safety checks and others differ.","The claim is about running and comparing checks on planted known flaws to catch problems in agent work, accepting that every checker has blind spots; that is E's 'run the check, don't wait for certainty'. It is not F's measuring whether a change improved the work with a fixed judge.","Planting known mistakes and measuring checkers' catch rate, false alarms and blind spots is about running a check because output alone can't show safety/correctness, not about eval fixed-judge, grounding, or other ideas.","Planting known mistakes, comparing checkers on precision, recall and coverage, and tracking misses is building a fixed, repeatable measurement (eval) of a judge, matching the 'eval with a fixed judge' idea.","The claim is about choosing a checker by testing candidates against planted mistakes and tracking what it misses. It says nothing about staying connected to truth (C), measuring whether a change improved the output with a fixed judge (D), or running a safety check (F)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Never trust one check: plant known errors, score several checks on your own output with precision and recall, and the winner becomes a reusable judge file. No single detector caught everything, and the one I'd have picked first missed things the others got.","claims":["When you need to detect problems in your own agent's output, don't rely on a single checker. Deliberately insert errors you know about, run several different checkers on the output, and measure each one on how many real problems it flags and how many of its flags are correct. Keep the best performer as a saved, reusable reviewer file. Don't assume your first-choice checker is best, because it can miss things others catch.","When you need to know whether your agent's work is any good, don't rely on a single checker. Insert mistakes you already know about into the output, run several candidate checkers on it, and measure each on how many of the planted mistakes it finds and how often its flags are right. Keep the best scorer as a saved, fixed reviewer you reuse. Don't assume your first-choice checker is the best, because it may miss things others catch.","Before relying on any one way of catching mistakes in your work, seed your output with errors you already know about. Run several different checkers over it and measure how many of the planted errors each one finds and how many of its flags are wrong. Pick the checker that does best and save it as a fixed, reusable file. Don't assume your favorite checker is the best one, because it may miss things the others catch.","When you need to find mistakes in your own work, don't rely on a single reviewer: deliberately insert mistakes you already know about, run several different review methods over the result, and measure how many real mistakes each one catches and how many of its alarms are false. Keep the best performer as a saved, reusable reviewer, and don't assume your favorite is the best until the numbers show it."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","none"],"why":["The claim is about building an eval: pick a judge by testing it against planted known mistakes, then keep it fixed and reuse it to measure the agent's work.","Planting known mistakes to score candidate judges, then keeping the best as a fixed, reusable judge, is about building a repeatable eval with a fixed judge to tell whether work is good.","Seeded known mistakes, several review methods, measured catch rate and false alarms, kept the best as a fixed reusable judge: that's an eval measured repeatably against criteria with a fixed judge.","The claim is about measuring reviewers against planted known errors (catch rate, false alarms) and keeping the best one as a fixed, reusable judge. That is the eval idea: criteria you define, repeatable measurement, a judge that stays fixed.","Seeding known errors, comparing checkers on hit and false-flag rates, and saving the best as a fixed reusable judge file is the eval idea: measure against defined criteria, repeatably, with a fixed judge.","The claim is about building a checker and validating it against known planted errors, then saving it as a fixed reusable file. That is the fixed-judge, repeatable-measurement idea behind evals, not safety checking (C) or grounding (E).","The claim is about building a judge: test several checkers against planted errors, measure each on recall and precision, and save the best as a fixed reviewer file. That is the eval idea of a repeatable fixed judge.","The claim is about choosing the best checker by seeding known errors and scoring each checker's recall and precision. That is neither grounding (A), nor 'run the check to tell if it's safe' (H), nor an eval with a fixed judge to compare changes (G)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"That an AI sounds just as sure when it's making things up, so asking it 'are you sure?' proves nothing; you have to build a check outside the agent, test it on your own material, and have a person verify against the source before anything important happens.","claims":["Don't trust an AI's own confidence or its reassurance when you ask it to double-check itself; it sounds equally certain when it's inventing things. Set up a separate check that doesn't rely on the AI's say-so, try it on your own real data first, and have a person compare the output to the original source before anything important depends on it.","When an AI tells me something important, I won't trust it just because it sounds confident or because it says yes when I ask whether it's right. I'll set up a separate checking step that doesn't rely on the AI's own word, try it first on our own real examples, and make sure a person compares the result to the original source before we act on it.","When an AI tells you something, its confident tone is no evidence it's right, and asking it to double-check itself won't help. Set up a separate check that doesn't rely on the AI, try it on your own real data first, and have a person compare the output to the original source before you act on anything important.","Don't trust an AI's own confidence or its reassurance when you ask it to double-check itself. Set up a separate check that runs outside the AI, try that check on your own real data first, and have a person compare important outputs to the original source before anything depends on them."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Claim says don't rely on the AI's self-assessment; build an independent check and run it, with human verification — matches 'can't tell safety from output; need a way to check, practice is running the check'.","The claim is about not trusting the AI's self-assessment and running an independent check (tested on real data, human compared to source) to know if it's safe to rely on.","The claim sets up an independent check, run first on real examples with a human comparing to source, rather than trusting the AI's confidence or self-assessment. That matches 'you can't tell from output; run the check.'","Claim sets up an independent check outside the AI's own word, tested on real examples, with a human comparing to source — running the check, not trusting confident output.","Claim says you can't trust output or self-check; run an independent check, test it, and have a human verify. That matches 'you can't tell if safe by looking at output; you need a way to check, and practice is running the check'.","The claim says output and confident tone can't show correctness, so you need an independent check and have to actually run it on real data. That is C's point that you can't judge an agent from its output and must run a check.","The claim says self-reported confidence can't show safety or correctness, so you need an external check that is actually run. That matches D. It is not G, because it is about verifying output rather than staying connected to ground truth.","Don't trust the AI's self-assessment; run an external check and verify outputs yourself, which is H's 'can't tell safety from output, run the check'."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Asking the AI 'are you sure?' proves nothing, because it sounds just as confident when it's wrong, so you need a separate check against sources, and you should test several checks on your own material rather than trust advice. The part I'd take to my leadership team is that the agent proposes, a human checks the evidence, and only then does it act.","claims":["When an AI tells you something, don't ask it to double-check itself, since it will sound equally sure whether right or wrong. Compare its output against the original documents or data instead, and try out a few different ways of doing that comparison on your own real material before relying on any recommended method. Set up work so the AI only suggests, a person reviews the evidence, and only after that does the action happen.","When an AI tells you it is right, don't take that as evidence, since it sounds equally sure whether it is correct or mistaken. Set up a separate check that compares its output to real source material, and try out a few different versions of that check on your own data to see which one catches errors. Have the AI propose an action, have a person review the evidence, and only then let it proceed.","Don't accept an AI's own 'I'm sure' as verification. Set up a separate check that compares its output against real sources, and try a few different checks on your own data to see which catches errors. Have the AI propose an action, have a person review the evidence, and only then let it proceed.","Don't accept an AI's own confirmation of its work, because it sounds equally sure whether it is right or wrong. Set up a separate check that compares its output against real source material, and try out a few different checks on your own data to see which catches mistakes. Have the AI propose an action, have a person review the evidence, and only then let it proceed."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","output-quality","security"],"why":["Output confidence can't show correctness, so the claim says to run an external check against the originals and test the check methods on your own material, with a human reviewing before action. That is 'you can't tell by looking at output; run the check'.","The claim says not to trust the AI's confident output or its self-check. It says to verify against the source, test the checking method on your own material, and keep a human review step before any action. That is 'you can't tell if it's safe from its output; run the check.'","The claim says the AI's confidence tells you nothing and you need a separate check you actually run, with a human reviewing before it proceeds. That is H's point that you can't judge safety from output alone.","The claim says the AI's confidence is not evidence and that you need a separate check and a human review step, which is F's point that you can't judge safety from output and must run a check.","Rejects the AI's self-assurance as verification and says to run actual checks (compare to sources, human review of evidence); B is about checking rather than trusting output or waiting for certainty, whereas E is about grounding to truth.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own confidence, to run separate checks, and to add a human review step. That is 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'. It never says the agent invents answers when it can't reach the real data, which is the grounding idea in A.","The claim centres on checking AI output against real source material because it sounds equally sure when wrong. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, and not trusting a confident gap-fill.","The claim says not to trust the AI's confident output, and to run separate checks and a human evidence review before proceeding. That is D's point that you can't judge safety from the output and have to run the check. It is not E, because it never mentions the agent filling gaps when it can't reach the ground truth."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"The difference between grounded and accurate: an AI answer can point to a source and still be wrong, so asking it 'are you sure?' checks nothing. For anything that matters, you need a separate check with known blind spots, and a named human who reviews before the agent acts.","claims":["Before an AI agent takes any action that matters, don't rely on the agent re-confirming its own answer, and don't treat a cited source as proof the answer is right. Run a separate verification step, and know in advance what kinds of mistakes that step tends to miss. Also name one specific person who reviews the result before the agent acts.","A source citation doesn't make an AI answer correct, and asking the AI to double-check itself proves nothing. When the result matters, run a second, independent check whose weak spots you know in advance, and have a specific named person review the result before the agent takes any action.","Don't trust an AI's answer just because it cites a source or says it's confident, and don't verify it by asking the same AI to double-check itself. For anything important, run a separate check built to catch a different kind of error than the AI makes, know what that check can't see, and make sure a specific named person signs off before the agent takes action.","An AI answer that cites a source can still be wrong, and asking the AI to confirm itself proves nothing. For anything important, have a separate check run (knowing it has its own blind spots), and name a specific person who reviews the result before the AI acts on it."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The colleague would act by running a separate check (with known blind spots) and naming a reviewer before the AI acts, rather than trusting the output or the AI's self-confirmation. That is G's 'run the check' practice, not F's idea of staying connected to ground truth.","The claim is that you can't trust the output or the AI's self-confirmation, so you run a separate check with a named human reviewer. That is F's 'run the check' idea, not H's grounding.","The claim says output and self-checks can't show the result is trustworthy, so you run an independent check and have a named person review before the agent acts. That is H's idea of running the check because output alone can't show safety.","The claim says output can't vouch for itself (a citation or self-check proves nothing), so you run an independent check and have a named person review it. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'.","The claim is about verifying with a separate check rather than trusting the agent's output or its own confidence, which is idea B (run the check). It is not G, which is about staying connected to ground truth.","Claim says output/citations/confidence can't establish trust and self-check is unreliable, so run an independent check and know its blind spots. That is idea A, you can't tell from output and must run the check. F (grounding) and H (evals) share words but aren't the point.","Claim says don't trust self-confirmation or citations, run a separate check aware of its blind spots, and have a named person review; this matches 'can't tell safety from output, you need a check and practice running it', not grounding (G).","Claim says run an independent check (not self-confirmation or citations) before acting, know its blind spots, and have a named reviewer: you can't tell safety from output, so run the check."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"You can't trust an AI's confidence: it sounds just as sure when it's wrong, so asking it 'are you sure?' is no check. Something outside the agent has to do the checking, and that means someone has to own the check.","claims":["Never accept an AI agent's own assurance that its work is right, and never ask it to re-confirm itself. Set up a separate check outside the agent (a test, a reviewer, or another tool), and name one specific person who is responsible for running it.","Don't treat the AI saying it's certain, or answering yes when you ask it to double-check itself, as proof its output is right. Have a separate check that isn't the same AI judging its own work, and assign a named person to be responsible for running it.","When an AI tells you its work is correct, don't treat that as verification, since it sounds equally certain whether right or wrong. Set up a separate check that runs outside the AI, and name a specific person who is responsible for running it.","When an AI tells you its work is correct, don't treat that as verification, because it sounds equally certain whether it is right or wrong and re-asking it changes nothing. Set up a check that runs outside the AI, and name a specific person who is responsible for running it."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Self-reported certainty or self-check isn't evidence of safety; you need a separate check and the practice is running it, with a named owner.","Both say not to trust the agent's own output or self-assurance as proof of safety/correctness; run an independent check as a practice, with a named owner.","Don't trust the agent's own output as proof of safety; run an external check, with the practice being to actually run it.","Both say not to trust the agent's own output: verify through an external check and make running it a practice. D's 'running the check' matches the claim's named owner who runs it.","The claim says the AI's own assurance isn't verification and calls for running an external check, which is D's idea that you can't tell safety from output and must run the check.","AI's self-reported correctness isn't verification; run an external check with a named owner, which is idea D (can't tell safety from output, run the check).","Output can't tell you if it's right or safe, so run an external check with an owner; this matches H's 'run the check' practice.","Claim says AI self-reports of correctness aren't verification, so run an external check with a named owner. That is B's 'can't tell from output, need a check, run it'."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust a brief because it sounds sure: break it into claims and check each against your sources with several different checks, because each catches different failures. Keep the best one as a judge file you can rerun on the next brief.","claims":["When an AI gives you a confident write-up, don't accept it as is. Split it into its individual statements and test each against your real sources, using more than one kind of test since each kind catches different mistakes. Save the most useful test as a reusable file so you can run it again on the next write-up.","When an agent hands you a confident-sounding brief, don't accept it as is. Split it into its individual factual statements and test each one against your real sources, using more than one kind of test since each kind catches different mistakes. Save the best test as a reusable file so you can run it again on the next brief.","When an agent hands you a confident-sounding brief, split it into its individual factual statements and verify each one against your own source material, using more than one kind of check since each kind catches different errors. Save the most effective check as a reusable file so you can run it again on the next brief.","When an AI hands you a confident write-up, don't accept it as is. Split it into its individual factual statements and verify each one against your own source material using more than one kind of test, since different tests catch different mistakes. Save the most effective test as a reusable file so you can run it again on the next write-up."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim is about not trusting a confident output by eye and running repeatable, varied checks on it (a reusable test file). That is G's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It isn't E, which is about keeping the agent connected to ground truth.","The claim's core act is not trusting a confident output and running checks on it (claim-by-claim, several test types, reusable), which is the practice of running the check. It says nothing about comparing changes with a fixed judge (C) or about connecting the agent to ground truth (E).","Checking each factual claim in a confident write-up against your own source material is grounding: stay connected to the truth, because a model fills gaps with confident, plausible, wrong text. The 'run several checks, keep the winner' detail matches the Module 5 page.","Splitting a write-up into factual statements and verifying each against source material is grounding: staying connected to the truth so confident but wrong gaps become visible.","The colleague would check a confident-sounding brief claim by claim against real sources, which is grounding. It isn't an eval of changes with a fixed judge, and it isn't about safety.","The claim is about checking an agent's confident brief claim by claim against real sources, which is grounding. Saving the best test is only a step in that practice.","Splitting a confident brief into claims and verifying each against source material is grounding: it makes the gap between the agent and the truth visible.","Splitting a brief into factual statements and verifying each against source material is grounding: it makes the gap between plausible claims and the truth visible."],"score":0.75,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust one check on your AI's output. Run several candidate checks on your own material, score them against claims you can trace to a source, and keep the one the numbers back. Then save it as a judge file with its limits written in, so a teammate can rerun it next week.","claims":["When you need to check whether your AI's work is good, don't rely on a single test. Try out several different tests on your own real examples, see which one best matches facts you can trace back to a source, and pick the one the results support. Write that test down as a reusable file that also states where it doesn't work, so a teammate can run it again next week.","When I need to check my AI's work, I should not rely on a single check. I should try several different checks on my own data, measure each against facts I can verify from a source, and pick the one that performs best. Then I should write that check down as a reusable file that states where it doesn't work, so a coworker can run it again later.","When you need to check an AI's work, don't rely on a single test. Try several different tests on your own data, compare each against facts you can trace back to a source, and pick the one whose results hold up. Write that test down in a file along with where it stops being reliable, so a coworker can run it again next week.","Before relying on any automated check of AI output, try several different checks against your own real material, and measure each one against cases where you can point to the source that proves the right answer. Pick the check whose results hold up in those numbers, then store it as a reusable file that states where it stops working, so someone else can run it again later."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The claim is about building a written, repeatable test with a fixed reference to measure the AI's output. It never mentions the agent filling gaps with confident wrong answers, so it isn't grounding (H).","Trying several tests, judging them against traceable facts, picking the one that holds up, and writing it down to rerun repeatably is building an eval with fixed criteria.","The claim is about building and running checks on the AI's work, then keeping the best one as a reusable file. It never says the agent should stay connected to ground truth or that its output fills gaps with confident errors (E). It also doesn't describe a fixed judge that tracks changes over time (H).","The claim is about building a fixed, repeatable judge: compare candidate checks against verifiable facts, keep the best, and write it down for reuse. That fits F better than D, since it never mentions the agent filling gaps with confident wrong answers.","Comparing several candidate tests against traceable facts, picking the best, and writing it as a reusable judge file with stated limits describes a repeatable eval with a fixed judge to tell whether work improved.","Trying several tests on real examples, picking the one that matches traceable facts, and saving it as a reusable file with stated limits is building a repeatable, fixed eval judge to measure output quality (the claim's grounding mention is only the criterion, not the idea).","The claim is about building a repeatable, fixed check (a judge) and validating it against known-right cases. That is the eval idea. It says nothing about grounding, safety, or sharing.","The claim is about choosing an automated check by validating it against known-right cases, then fixing it as a reusable, repeatable file. That is eval and judge practice, not safety (G) or grounding (H)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust a recommended way of checking the agent's work. Plant known errors in your own material, run several checks against them, and keep the one the numbers favour as a saved judge file that says what it catches and what it misses.","claims":["Don't just accept someone's suggested method for reviewing the agent's output. Deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into your own sample material, try several different review approaches on it, and see which one finds the most of them. Save the best reviewer as a file that states which kinds of mistakes it catches and which it misses, and use that one from then on.","Before relying on any suggested way of reviewing your agent's output, test it: insert mistakes you already know about into your own sample material, run a few candidate review methods on it, and compare how many planted mistakes each one finds. Pick the method with the best results and save it as a file that states which kinds of problems it reliably finds and which it misses.","When you need a way to check your agent's work, don't adopt the one someone recommends. Insert mistakes you already know about into your own material, try several candidate checkers on it, and see which one finds the most. Keep the best one as a file that states which kinds of mistakes it finds and which it misses, and use that file as your checker from then on.","When you need a way to check your agent's work, don't adopt whichever one is recommended. Insert mistakes you already know about into your own material, try several candidate checks on it, and count how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Pick the one that finds the most, and save it as a file that states which kinds of mistakes it finds and which it misses, so you can reuse it."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","none","evaluations"],"why":["Seeding known errors in sample material, comparing review approaches on them, and keeping the best judge as a fixed, documented file is eval-building: a repeatable, fixed judge whose quality is validated against known criteria.","Seeding known errors, comparing reviewers, and saving the best one as a fixed file that states what it catches and misses is how you build and calibrate a repeatable judge, which is the eval idea.","Planting known mistakes to compare candidate checks and saving the winner as a reusable judge file is the eval idea: criteria you define, repeatable, fixed judge, measuring whether work gets better.","Planting known mistakes and scoring candidate checks on them, then saving a reusable judge file, is about measuring and fixing a repeatable eval/judge; E is about running a check, but the claim's core is choosing a judge by measured detection.","Testing review methods against planted known mistakes and saving the best as a judge with documented coverage is eval-building: fixed, repeatable measurement of output quality.","Planting known mistakes, comparing candidate review methods, and saving the best one as a fixed, documented judge is the eval idea: a repeatable, fixed judge you've validated against criteria you define.","The claim is about choosing a checker by testing candidates on mistakes you planted. That isn't safety-checking (A), grounding (C), or measuring whether a change improved the work (E).","The claim is about validating a checker on planted known errors and then fixing it as a file to reuse, which is the eval idea of a repeatable, fixed judge. It isn't about grounding or safety checks."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"A long agent workflow with no checks can be mostly wrong even when every step looks fine, because wrong output sounds as confident as right output. So you need checks, and several kinds, since each one catches different errors than the others.","claims":["When you chain many agent steps, don't trust the result just because each step reads well. Add several different checks along the way, such as comparing against a source, re-running the work, and having a second reviewer, because each one catches mistakes the others miss.","When you hand an agent a long multi-step job, don't trust the final result just because each step read fine. Put several different kinds of verification into the workflow (for example a test run, a second reviewer, a comparison against a known source), because each kind catches mistakes the others miss, and wrong answers sound just as sure as right ones.","When an agent does a long job in many steps, don't trust the result just because each step looked fine. Put in several different verification points, each built to catch a different kind of mistake, because a wrong answer reads just as sure of itself as a right one.","When an agent does a long job in many steps, don't trust the result just because each step looked fine; it can sound just as sure when it's wrong. Build in several different kinds of verification along the way, because each kind catches mistakes the others miss."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim says not to trust output that reads fine and to run several independent checks; that is G's idea that you can't judge from the output and must run the check.","The claim says you can't judge a result by how it reads, so you should run several different checks. That is idea G (can't tell from the output, run the check). It does not teach E's point about staying connected to ground truth.","The claim says output that reads well can't be trusted and you have to run checks. That is C. It doesn't carry D's point about staying connected to ground truth and the gap being filled confidently.","Several different independent checks run along the way, rather than trusting output that reads well, is the 'run the check, you can't tell from output' idea.","The claim says you can't judge an agent by how its output looks or how sure it sounds, so you have to run checks (several kinds). That is H, not G, because it says nothing about connecting the agent to ground truth.","The claim says not to trust output that looks fine or sounds sure, and to run verification checks. That is E's idea that you can't judge an agent from its output and have to run the check.","The claim says not to trust output on looks, because wrong answers sound as confident as right ones, so build and run several checks. That is idea D: you can't judge by looking at the output, so you run the check.","Several distinct verification points, since confident output can't reveal wrongness, matches 'you can't tell if safe/correct from output, you need to run a check' (M4); B is about grounding in truth, not layered checks."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust the AI just because it sounds sure. Check what it wrote against the real file, like a depot manager saying 'all loaded' and you still look. The rest was too long and I don't remember it well.","claims":["When an AI tells you a piece of work is finished or correct, don't take its word for it. Open the actual file or source yourself and compare it with what the AI said before you rely on it.","When an AI tells you a task is finished or correct, don't take its word for it. Open the actual file or output yourself and compare it to what the AI said before you rely on it.","When an AI tells you a task is finished or correct, don't accept its word. Open the actual file or output yourself and compare it with what the AI said it did.","When an AI tells you a piece of work is finished or correct, don't take its word for it. Open the actual file or output yourself and compare it to what the AI said it did before you rely on it."],"picks":["security","output-quality","security","security","security","security","none","security"],"why":["The claim says not to trust the AI's own report and to run a check against the real file yourself. That is D's idea of running the check instead of judging from output. B is about keeping the agent connected to sources, which the claim doesn't mention.","Verifying the AI's 'done/correct' claim against the actual file or source is grounding: staying connected to the truth rather than trusting what the agent fills in.","The claim says to verify an AI's 'done/correct' by running a check (opening the actual output) rather than trusting it, which matches 'you can't tell it's safe/correct from output alone; run the check'.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own report of success, and to check the actual file or output yourself. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'. It is not about grounding the agent in truth (B), and it is not about repeatable evals (C).","The claim is to verify by opening the actual output yourself rather than trusting the AI's word, which is running a check instead of relying on output or self-report.","The claim says to verify an agent's work by checking it yourself instead of trusting its report, which matches F's 'run the check' practice.","The claim is a human spot-check of an AI's 'done' report against the real file. B is about safety checks that go beyond looking at output, C is about the agent staying grounded, and H is about repeatable evals with a fixed judge.","The claim is to check the work yourself instead of trusting the agent's self-report; F says you can't judge safety from output alone and have to run a check."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Do not trust AI text just because it sounds sure. Make a second AI check it against your own files, and asking 'are you sure' does not work. I would have liked an example from a depot.","claims":["When an AI gives you written output that sounds confident, don't accept it as is. Have a separate AI pass check that output against your own documents and data. Asking the same AI whether it is sure is not a real check, so don't rely on it.","When an AI gives you a confident answer, don't accept it on tone alone, and don't just ask it whether it's certain. Have a separate AI session verify the answer against your own documents and data, and only rely on the answer if that check supports it.","When an AI gives you a confident written answer, don't accept it on tone, and don't just ask it to confirm itself. Have a separate AI run a check of that answer against your own documents and data.","When an AI gives you written output that sounds confident, don't accept it on tone. Have a separate AI pass check that output against your own source documents, and don't rely on asking the same AI whether it is certain."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Checking confident-sounding output against your own documents/data is grounding: staying connected to truth so confident gaps get exposed.","The claim is about confident-sounding output being checked against your own documents and data, which is staying connected to the ground truth. It isn't about general safety checks.","The claim is about not trusting confident answers and checking them against your own documents and data, which is grounding.","Claim is about not trusting confident answers and verifying them against your own documents/data (staying connected to ground truth), which is grounding; H is about safety checks, not factual verification.","Don't trust output on tone or self-confirmation; run a separate check against your own data, which is the 'run the check' practice. G is about grounding but the claim centers on running an independent check.","The claim is about not trusting confident output on tone or self-confirmation and running a separate check of it, which is idea C's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. D is about a fixed judge measuring whether a change improved the work. H is about grounding.","Confident-sounding output can't be trusted on tone; checking against source documents (the ground) is the grounding idea, not a generic safety check.","The claim is about confident-sounding output being unreliable and needing a check against your own source documents, which is grounding in the truth. B is about safety checks, so it fits less well."],"score":0.75,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"AI sounds just as sure when it makes things up, and asking 'are you sure?' does not fix it, so you have to check the output against a source. Rest was too much English and software words for me.","claims":["When an AI gives you an answer, don't trust it because it sounds confident, and don't try to settle it by asking it to double-check itself. Compare what it produced against a real source you can look at yourself.","When an AI gives you an answer, don't trust it because it sounds confident, and don't rely on asking it to double-check itself. Compare what it produced against a real, independent source before you use it.","When an AI gives you an answer, don't trust it because it sounds confident, and don't ask it whether it is certain. Compare what it said against a real source you can look up yourself.","When an AI gives you an answer, don't trust it because it sounds confident, and don't trust it because it says yes when you ask if it's certain. Compare what it produced against a real, trustworthy source before you rely on it."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Comparing output against a real source you can check, rather than trusting confident-sounding answers or self-review, is grounding: staying connected to the truth.","Compare output against a real source you can see (stay connected to truth) rather than trusting confident-sounding output is the grounding idea.","Distrusting confident output and checking against an independent real source is grounding: staying connected to truth rather than trusting a confident gap-fill.","Confident-sounding output isn't evidence; the claim says to check against an independent real source, which is grounding in truth outside the model.","Don't trust confident-sounding answers; check against a real source (the ground) — grounding, not just verification via a run-the-check practice.","The claim is about not trusting confident output and checking against a real source, which matches grounding: staying connected to truth because the agent confidently fills gaps.","Checking the AI's confident output against a real source you can look up is grounding: staying connected to the truth rather than trusting plausible-sounding answers.","Checking the AI's answer against a real source you can look up is grounding: staying connected to the truth rather than trusting confident output."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"AI writes what sounds right, not what is true, so it can make up numbers. Asking it 'are you sure' does not help, you have to check against the source.","claims":["When an AI gives you facts or figures, don't trust them because they sound right, and don't ask the AI to confirm itself. Look each one up in the original source yourself before you use it.","When an AI gives you facts or figures, don't trust them just because they read well and don't settle it by asking the AI to confirm itself. Look up the real source and compare the numbers against it yourself.","When the AI gives you figures, names or facts, look each one up in the original document or data yourself before you rely on it. Do not ask the AI to confirm its own answer, because it will just restate it with confidence.","When the AI gives you facts or figures, don't trust them because they read well, and don't ask it to re-confirm them. Look each one up in the original source yourself before you use it."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","security","output-quality"],"why":["The claim is about verifying AI facts against the original source rather than trusting plausible-sounding output, which is grounding: staying connected to truth.","Verifying AI facts against the original source is staying connected to ground truth, since the AI fills gaps confidently and plausibly wrong.","Don't trust fluent AI facts or self-confirmation; check against the real source — this is grounding (stay connected to truth).","Fluent-but-unchecked facts, with self-confirmation rejected, and the fix being to look up the real source, is the grounding idea: stay connected to truth.","Verifying figures yourself against the source, rather than trusting the AI's self-confirmation, is the idea of running an independent check because output alone can't show whether it's right.","The claim says you can't trust the AI's confident output or its self-confirmation, so you run an independent check against the source. That is idea H's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. E is about keeping the agent connected to the ground truth, not about the human verifying its output.","The claim says you can't judge facts by how well they read, and asking the AI to re-confirm isn't a real check. The action is to run an independent check against the original source, which is idea A.","Verifying facts against the original source yourself is grounding: staying connected to the truth because the AI fills gaps with plausible-sounding but wrong content."],"score":0.625,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust a method because someone sells it: plant known errors, let several checks compete on your own material, and read the scoreboard yourself. A confident agent tone says nothing about whether it's right.","claims":["Before adopting any approach for checking or improving your work, test it on your own material with mistakes you've deliberately inserted, run several competing alternatives against each other, and look at the results yourself. Don't take an agent's assured tone as evidence that its output is correct.","Before adopting any method or tool someone is pitching, test it yourself: seed your own material with mistakes you already know about, run several different checks against that material, and look at the results directly. Never treat a sure-sounding tone from an AI as a sign it's correct.","Before adopting any approach, test it yourself: seed your own material with mistakes you already know about, run several competing checks on it, and look at which ones actually catch the mistakes. Don't take the seller's word, and don't treat a self-assured tone from the agent as evidence that its output is correct.","Before adopting any method for judging or improving agent work, test it on your own material: seed errors you already know about, run several competing checks, and look at the results yourself. Don't treat a confident-sounding output as evidence that it's correct."],"picks":["none","evaluations","evaluations","security","none","security","security","none"],"why":["The claim is about validating the checking method itself (planted errors, competing alternatives, your own inspection). A says to run a check on agent output, and E says to measure with a fixed judge, but neither covers testing the checker.","Testing approaches on seeded mistakes, comparing alternatives and checking results yourself is the eval idea: measure output against fixed criteria so you know a change helped.","Seeding known mistakes, running multiple checks, and not trusting confident tone is about building a repeatable eval/check with known ground truth. A's 'can't tell by looking at output; run the check' is close, but G's measurable, repeatable-judge framing fits the seeded-test practice best.","Seeding known mistakes, running several checks and inspecting results, and not trusting confident tone is 'you can't tell by looking at output; run the check' (A), not a fixed-judge eval for measuring change (D) or grounding (G).","The claim is about validating a checker by seeding known errors and comparing which competing checks catch them. B is about measuring with a fixed judge, and E is about the need to run a check at all, so neither is this idea.","The claim says confident output isn't evidence of correctness and you have to run checks, seeded with known errors, to see which ones catch them. That is G's point that output alone can't tell you, so you need to run the check.","The claim says not to trust confident-sounding output and to run checks, seeded with known errors, on your own material. That matches C's 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'. It doesn't mention truth or grounding (B), and it isn't about a fixed judge for measuring change (H).","The claim is about validating a judging method itself, by seeding known errors and comparing competing checks. A defines what an eval is, E is about running a check on agent safety, and G is about grounding. None of them carries that idea."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't take an AI's word that its output is good, including its own 'are you sure?'. Seed some deliberate errors into your own material, score a few checkers against that answer key, and keep the one that catches the most, along with the list of what it misses.","claims":["When you need to know whether an AI's output is right, don't accept its own assurance, even when you ask it to double-check. Plant mistakes you know about in a sample of your own work, run several candidate reviewers on it, and pick the reviewer that finds the most planted mistakes. Also write down which kinds of mistakes that reviewer still misses, so you know where to check by hand.","Before trusting any automated reviewer, plant a set of known mistakes in your own documents, run several candidate reviewers over them, and pick the one that finds the most planted mistakes. Also keep a written list of the mistake types it still misses, and never rely on the AI vouching for itself.","When you need to know whether an AI's work is any good, don't accept its own say-so or a re-check from the same AI. Plant known mistakes in material where you already know the right answers, run several candidate reviewers on it, and pick the one that finds the most planted mistakes. Also write down which kinds of mistakes it still misses, so you know where to check by hand.","When you need to know whether an AI's work is any good, don't accept its own say-so, even when you ask it to double-check. Plant known mistakes in your own material, run several candidate reviewers over it, and pick the one that finds the most planted mistakes. Keep a written list of the kinds it still misses so you know where to check by hand."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations"],"why":["Planting known mistakes and comparing candidate reviewers to pick the best is building and validating a repeatable eval/judge, not just grounding or safety-checking.","Planting known mistakes, comparing reviewers on a fixed set, and documenting blind spots is building a repeatable eval/judge measured against criteria you define, not trusting the AI's self-assurance.","Planting known mistakes, comparing candidate reviewers, and tracking misses is building a fixed, repeatable judge to measure output quality, which is the eval idea.","Planting known mistakes and comparing candidate reviewers against a fixed measure is building a repeatable eval with a calibrated judge, not just distrusting the agent's self-check.","Planting known mistakes in answer-known material to pick the best reviewer is building a fixed, repeatable judge to measure output quality, which is the eval idea.","Planting known mistakes in material with known answers to measure and select a reviewer, plus documenting its misses, is building a repeatable eval with a fixed, validated judge to measure output quality.","The claim is about not trusting a reviewer or the AI's own word for it, and about running an actual check (planted mistakes) to see whether it works. That matches 'you can't tell by looking; run the check'. It doesn't carry grounding (E) or the fixed-judge eval idea (G).","Planting known mistakes and comparing candidate reviewers on a fixed test set, with a written list of misses, is building a repeatable, fixed measurement of the judge's quality, which is the eval idea. Not G, which is about running a check on safety."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust a check because it sounds sure; plant known errors, run several checkers against the answer key, and read the scores, because no single one catches everything. And whatever check you keep should say what it cannot catch.","claims":["Before you rely on any automated reviewer, test it: slip in mistakes you already know about, run a few different reviewers on the same material, and compare what each one found against your list. Expect each to miss some. For whichever reviewer you keep, write down which kinds of mistakes it won't find, so nobody treats its approval as proof.","Before relying on any reviewing step, test it: deliberately insert mistakes you already know about, run several different reviewers on the result, and compare what each one found against your list. Don't assume one reviewer is enough, since each misses some. Then write down, for the reviewer you keep, which kinds of mistakes it fails to detect.","Before relying on any automated reviewer, seed the material with mistakes you already know about, run a few different reviewers on it, and compare what each found against your list. Don't pick one because it sounds confident; none will find everything. For the reviewer you keep, write down which kinds of mistakes it misses.","When you rely on an automated reviewer to catch mistakes, first test it: deliberately insert errors you already know about, run a few different reviewers on the same material, and compare each one's results to the list of planted errors. Don't believe a reviewer just because it sounds confident, since each misses different things. For the reviewer you keep, write down what kinds of mistakes it can't find."],"picks":["security","evaluations","security","security","evaluations","security","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The claim is about checking the reviewing step itself: plant known flaws, run several reviewers, and record what each misses. That is the 'you can't trust the output, run the check' idea, not measuring whether a change improved the work.","Testing a reviewer against known inserted mistakes and keeping a fixed, characterized judge is the eval idea: measure repeatably with a judge you've validated.","Seeding known mistakes and comparing reviewers' catches tests the check itself, and documenting blind spots means approval isn't proof of safety — the practice of running the check rather than trusting output.","The claim is about not treating a reviewer's approval as proof and checking the checker with planted known flaws, which is the idea that output alone can't show safety and you have to run a check.","The claim is about validating the judge itself: plant known errors, compare reviewers against them, and record the blind spots of the one you keep. That is eval and judge calibration, not grounding or safety checks.","The claim is about not trusting a confident reviewer and running a real check against planted known errors, then recording its blind spots. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check', not measuring whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about validating a judge before trusting it: seed known flaws, compare reviewers, pick one on evidence, and record its blind spots. That is the eval idea of a fixed, tested judge.","Seeding known mistakes and comparing reviewers against a fixed answer key is building a repeatable eval with a fixed standard, so you can judge whether a reviewer works. Recording its blind spots is part of that."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust the AI's own say-so that its output is right: plant known errors, run several checkers against them, and read the scores to see which one actually catches things. Whether it changes anything on Monday, I still don't know.","claims":["When you want to know whether an AI's work is correct, don't accept its own statement that it is. Deliberately seed the material with mistakes you already know about, run each of several reviewers over it, and compare which reviewer finds the seeded mistakes. Rely on the reviewer that catches them.","Don't accept an AI's statement that its own work is correct. Seed the work with mistakes you already know about, have several different reviewers check it, and compare how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Then rely on the reviewer that catches the most.","Don't accept an AI's statement that its own work is correct. Deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into the material, have each of several reviewers examine it, and compare which reviewers find the planted mistakes. Rely on the reviewer that catches them.","Don't accept an AI's statement that its own work is correct. Deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into the material, have each of several reviewers examine it, and compare how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Then rely on the reviewer that catches the most."],"picks":["none","evaluations","security","evaluations","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is about calibrating a reviewer by seeding known defects and picking the one that catches them. D says to run a check but not how to validate the checker, and E is about a fixed judge measuring whether a change improved the work.","Seeding known mistakes and comparing reviewers to pick one that reliably catches them is building a repeatable, fixed-criteria measure of work quality, which is the eval idea; it isn't about safety checks (G) or grounding (F).","The claim says not to trust an AI's own verdict on its work and to run an independent check. Planting known mistakes and comparing reviewers is how you do that. It isn't about judging whether a change improved the work (H).","Planting known mistakes and comparing reviewers' catch rates is a repeatable measurement against defined criteria that tells you which judge to trust. That is the eval idea, a fixed, validated judge, not just 'verify the output' (F).","The claim says not to trust the output or the AI's own say-so, and to run a concrete check (planted known errors) to find a reviewer that works. That is D's 'run the check, don't wait for certainty', not A's measuring whether a change improved the work.","Planting known errors and seeing which reviewer catches them is a way to check the agent's work rather than trusting its self-assessment; that matches 'you need a way to check, run the check'. H is the nearest alternative, but it concerns measuring output against criteria with a fixed judge, not validating reviewers with planted flaws.","The claim says not to trust an AI's self-assessment and to run a concrete check instead. It does this by planting known errors and measuring each reviewer's catch rate. It doesn't mention F's repeatable measurement of whether a change improved the work.","The claim says not to trust an AI's own verdict on its work and to run a concrete check instead (planted errors, compare catch rates). That is A's idea that you can't judge by looking at output and have to run a check. D is about a fixed judge measuring changes, which the claim doesn't cover."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
